  <content>
  <section><para>In this lesson, you will work with problems that show patterns of change and be able to describe the patterns in mathematical terms. A new term, quadratic, will be used to describe patterns with squared terms.</para>
<para>When you finish this lesson, you will be able to:</para>
<list>
  <item> Describe how a pattern is growing. </item>
  <item> Tell whether a pattern is growing linearly, exponentially, or quadratically. </item>
  <item> Explain that an expression with a squared term is called quadratic. </item>
</list>
<para>Here are the <emphasis>activities</emphasis> that will help you reach those goals:</para>
<list>
  <item> 7.2.1: Squares in a Geometric Pattern </item>
  <item> 7.2.2: Constant and Exponential Change </item>
  <list>
    <item> 7.2.2: Self Check </item>
    <item> 7.2.2: Additional Resources </item>
  </list>
  <item> 7.2.3: Quadratic Expressions </item>
  <list>
    <item> 7.2.3: Self Check </item>
    <item> 7.2.3: Additional Resources </item>
  </list>
  <item> 7.2.4: Quadratic Relationships </item>
</list>
<para>After that, you’ll<emphasis> practice and review</emphasis>.</para>
<list>
  <item> 7.2.5: Practice </item>
  <item> 7.2.6: Lesson Summary </item>
</list></section>
</content>
